// T4.0 — the ask eval set. 10 gold questions + 3 decoys, scored by evals/ask-run.ts.
// `plans/06 §ask evals`: (a) every emitted claim has a citation that exists and says what the
// claim says — 100% or the build fails; (b) gold coverage >= 7/10; (c) --strict on an
// unanswerable question returns the refusal, not a guess (3 decoys).
//
// Corpus: the committed synthetic fixture evals/fixture/claude — 46 sessions, 6 sidechains,
// 12 ghosts, 33 cards, 8 projects named -tmp-potsherd-eval-*. Synthetic and redacted, so the
// questions and the quotes are safe to commit and CI can run on them. Every gold answer was
// located in the raw transcript before the question was written; the file, the seq and the
// quote are in evals/ASK-EVALS.md. Every decoy was proved absent by a grep, also pasted there.
//
// This set was written by a worker who could not see packages/core/src/ask.ts and did not read
// it. That separation is the whole point: in phase 1 one worker wrote both the queries and the
// ranker and produced a meaningless 10/10.
//
// Gold line:
//   id              g01..g10
//   question        the way somebody remembers the conversation weeks later, not the way they
//                   typed it at the time
//   expectSessions  full session ids. A sidechain is "<parent>:agent-<hash>" and the PARENT IS
//                   NOT LISTED when the answer is only in the subagent — listing it would let an
//                   answer that never read the subagent count as covered
//   expectSeqs      [sessionId, seq] pairs that physically hold the answer. Exchange seq is
//                   1-based; ghost_prompts seq is 0-based. Reported, not gated: it is the number
//                   that says whether the reader found the right exchange or only the right
//                   session
//   expectPhrases   regex alternatives, matched case-insensitively against result.answer. At
//                   least one must match. Each alternative is a distinct legitimate wording of
//                   the same fact, never a second fact that would make the gate easier
//   gold            the hand-written answer, from the transcript
//   note            why the question is fair, what the distractors are, and how the answer was
//                   verified
//   overlap         max(text, card) content-word overlap with the answer sessions, measured.
//                   ask-run.ts re-measures it every run and FAILS if this number is wrong by
//                   more than 0.02 — a set cannot lie about its own overlap
//
// Decoy line: {id, question, decoy:true, note:<the grep proving nothing answers it>}.
//
// Shapes covered: 3 sidechain-only, 2 ghost-only, 2 card, 2 multi-session, 1 ordinary exchange.
{"id": "g01", "question": "how many requests a second did the checkout endpoint hold before the tail latency blew up", "expectSessions": ["6b3a9e24-8f05-4c31-b7d2-1a94e6035cb7:agent-p4a"], "expectSeqs": [["6b3a9e24-8f05-4c31-b7d2-1a94e6035cb7:agent-p4a", 1]], "expectPhrases": ["380|three hundred and eighty"], "gold": "It held to three hundred and eighty a second. Past that the ninety-ninth percentile went from a hundred and twenty milliseconds to four seconds, because the connection wait queue never drained.", "note": "SIDECHAIN-ONLY. The parent 6b3a9e24 designs the ramp (fifty virtual users a minute up to a thousand) and names what to watch, but never reports a number; the number exists only in subagents/agent-p4a.jsonl. The parent is deliberately NOT in expectSessions, so an answer that reads only the parent scores uncovered. Distractors: c8e14f60 and ea4d7c60 are both about payload weight and say nothing about throughput; 19f2c8d1 is the other 'endpoint is slow' session. Verified: grep -rl 'three hundred and eighty a second' returns exactly one file, the subagent transcript.", "overlap": 0.13}
{"id": "g02", "question": "how much weight came off once we stopped pulling the whole icon package in one go", "expectSessions": ["4ae3102b-1ad3-432b-8f01-b0e64eb5d627:agent-s6a"], "expectSeqs": [["4ae3102b-1ad3-432b-8f01-b0e64eb5d627:agent-s6a", 1]], "expectPhrases": ["412"], "gold": "412 KB. Tree shaking removes 412 KB of the icon set once the barrel import is replaced with per-icon imports, and the date library drops another 288 KB after switching to the modular build.", "note": "SIDECHAIN-ONLY. The parent 4ae3102b says only 'three quarters of it is the date library and the icon set. Both are importable per-symbol' — no kilobytes. The measurement is in subagents/agent-s6a.jsonl. Distractors are strong and on-topic: ea4d7c60 says the date library is 400 kB, c8e14f60 says the hero image is 800 kB and 1b6f0d95 is about the icon sprite; none of them says 412. expectPhrases is the single number, so an answer that waves at 'a few hundred kilobytes' fails. Verified: grep -rl '412 KB of the icon set' returns exactly one file.", "overlap": 0.22}
{"id": "g03", "question": "why did the big amounts stop being exact after the loader switch", "expectSessions": ["2f7c8b31-6d40-4a75-9e13-c0b5827af4d6:agent-p2a"], "expectSeqs": [["2f7c8b31-6d40-4a75-9e13-c0b5827af4d6:agent-p2a", 1]], "expectPhrases": ["\\bdouble\\b", "ten million|10 million", "int32"], "gold": "The parquet writer emits the amount column as a double while the source keeps it as a numeric with two decimal places, so above about ten million the pennies are rounded away. The same pass also found the status enum arriving as a bare int32 with no dictionary.", "note": "SIDECHAIN-ONLY, and the set's cleanest concept question — zero shared content words with its answer. The parent 2f7c8b31 is about moving the warehouse loader to parquet and never mentions precision, doubles or amounts; the schema diff is in subagents/agent-p2a.jsonl. Distractor: b8f43d05 is the other 'the amounts came out wrong' session and is a text-versus-numeric join fault, not a float fault, so an answer citing b8f43d05 is wrong and scores uncovered. Three phrase alternatives because a correct answer may name the type, the threshold or the enum; none of the three appears in the parent. Verified: grep -rl 'rounds the pennies away' returns exactly one file.", "overlap": 0.00}
{"id": "g04", "question": "how were we going to keep the billing job from holding up the backup overnight", "expectSessions": ["4ddd4b1f-8f16-40c8-8970-658738871ba0"], "expectSeqs": [["4ddd4b1f-8f16-40c8-8970-658738871ba0", 0], ["4ddd4b1f-8f16-40c8-8970-658738871ba0", 1]], "expectPhrases": ["\\bqueue\\b", "in parallel"], "gold": "Move the billing cron onto a queue so it can run in parallel, instead of letting it run for forty minutes and block the nightly backup. Only the prompts survive — the session is a ghost — and it leaves an open question: how many workers before the payment gateway rate limit is hit.", "note": "GHOST-ONLY. There is no assistant side at all: the four prompts in history.jsonl are the entire record, and the plan is stated in the second prompt. The ghost has no card either, so nothing but the prompt text carries it. Strong distractor: 4c07b9e3 is a LIVE session titled 'The replica backup outruns its window' and says both 'backup' and 'window', and 3d92c7fe's card calls its subject 'the nightly deployment pipeline'; neither is the answer. The two shared words (billing, backup) are the unavoidable subject nouns — the answer itself (a queue, in parallel) shares nothing with the question. Verified: grep on history.jsonl returns the four prompts, quoted in ASK-EVALS.md.", "overlap": 0.29}
{"id": "g05", "question": "why did the app store send the build back, and did we ever get it published", "expectSessions": ["f1c48b07-6d92-4a35-8e07-2b91c5d4703a"], "expectSeqs": [["f1c48b07-6d92-4a35-8e07-2b91c5d4703a", 0], ["f1c48b07-6d92-4a35-8e07-2b91c5d4703a", 3]], "expectPhrases": ["privacy label", "advertising id"], "gold": "It was rejected for a missing privacy label. The thread went looking for which SDK was collecting the advertising id and whether the app could ship without it, then resubmitted with the label filled in and it went through. Ghost session: the four prompts survive, the assistant side does not.", "note": "GHOST-ONLY, and the second half of the question ('did we ever get it published') is answerable only from the fourth prompt — an answer that stops at the rejection is incomplete. No card exists for this ghost. Distractor: 8d31f7c5 is the other mobile release-process session (duplicate push notifications) and c93f1e07 is the other 'the app did something twice' one. DEFENDED at 0.50: the three shared words are app, store and build, which are the subject of the question and cannot be asked around; every word of the answer — privacy label, advertising id, resubmitted — is unshared. Verified: grep on history.jsonl returns the four prompts, quoted in ASK-EVALS.md.", "overlap": 0.50}
{"id": "g06", "question": "why could people not log in after coming back from the identity provider", "expectSessions": ["e5a70c14-9b38-4d52-a670-5c39f28b1e64"], "expectSeqs": [["e5a70c14-9b38-4d52-a670-5c39f28b1e64", 1], ["e5a70c14-9b38-4d52-a670-5c39f28b1e64", 2]], "expectPhrases": ["samesite", "partitioned"], "gold": "The cookie was set with SameSite=Lax and the cross-site POST dropped it on the way back; None with Secure survives the hop. Safari additionally wants Partitioned, set on the same response that performs the redirect. The 2019 webview ignores Partitioned and falls through to the header path, which still works.", "note": "NEEDS THE CARD. The transcript never contains the words log in, sign in, authentication, login, cookie or identity — it opens 'after the redirect back from the provider the browser is not sending it', and the title is 'SameSite and the third-party redirect'. Only the card names it: 'Users could not sign in through the identity provider because the authentication cookie was rejected on the way back.' Without cards_fts/vec_cards in the shortlist this question is not findable by the words a user would actually use. Distractors: f09b6c73 is the other iOS-Safari session and 3e9a7b15 is the other 'everything started failing this morning' one. DEFENDED at 0.50: the overlap is entirely with the card, which is the point of the shape; the answer's substance (Lax, None, Secure, Partitioned, the header path) is in the exchanges and in expectPhrases.", "overlap": 0.50}
{"id": "g07", "question": "what went wrong with the finance report and did we ever reconcile march", "expectSessions": ["b8f43d05-1e79-4a26-8c40-6b271fa9d3e5"], "expectSeqs": [["b8f43d05-1e79-4a26-8c40-6b271fa9d3e5", 1], ["b8f43d05-1e79-4a26-8c40-6b271fa9d3e5", 3]], "expectPhrases": ["\\bcast", "text .{0,40}numeric|numeric .{0,40}text"], "gold": "The join compared a text column against a numeric one, so every row fell out to the outer side and the amounts published blank. The loader now casts at load time; March was re-run with the cast in place and the row count agreed with the ledger. Earlier quarters had the same fault — the blanks were read as zeroes downstream and nobody looked.", "note": "NEEDS THE CARD. The transcript says only 'the march file has blanks where the amounts should be' and never uses the words finance, report, monthly or reconcile; the card supplies all four ('Type mismatch broke the monthly finance report ... March was regenerated and reconciled'). Distractors: 7f3b2d48 is the other 'the export went wrong' data session, d80a3f16 is the other reporting-correctness one, and g03's parquet session is the other 'the amounts are wrong' one. DEFENDED at 0.50: the shared words (march, finance, report) are all in the card, not the transcript; the transcript-only overlap is 0.17. The second expectSeq (seq 3) is what makes the 'did we reconcile' half real.", "overlap": 0.50}
{"id": "g08", "question": "how long do we hang on to the ids that stop a repeat from being counted twice", "expectSessions": ["7c1d0e44-2b96-4f31-a0d7-5e1c9b2a4f83", "47d9e281-3b64-4c07-a9f8-25e1d0b73c6a", "c93f1e07-6d24-4b85-a710-5f28be03c9d1"], "expectSeqs": [["7c1d0e44-2b96-4f31-a0d7-5e1c9b2a4f83", 3], ["47d9e281-3b64-4c07-a9f8-25e1d0b73c6a", 1], ["c93f1e07-6d24-4b85-a710-5f28be03c9d1", 2]], "expectPhrases": ["twenty-four hours|24 hours", "seven days|7 days|seven-day"], "gold": "Three different windows, one per place, and nothing in the corpus agrees on a single number. Idempotency keys on the charge endpoint are held for twenty-four hours. The analytics rollup keeps a message id in a seen-ids table with a seven-day ttl. On mobile the server remembers an in-flight analytics batch id for a day.", "note": "MULTI-SESSION, three of them, in three different projects (api, data, mobile). No single session answers it: each holds exactly one of the three retentions. plans/06's coverage rule only requires one expected session in the evidence, so this question can be scored covered on a third of the answer — ask-run.ts therefore also prints a non-gating 'all sessions cited' column, which is the number to read for this row. Distractors that look identical and are not the answer: 2d6b91ae (unique index on the provider transfer id, no ttl), c47b1a09 (a seven day tombstone ttl, which is sync, not deduplication) and 0c68f4a1 (voids a duplicate capture, no id retention at all). c47b1a09 is the trap: it says 'seven day ttl' and is the wrong session.", "overlap": 0.43}
{"id": "g09", "question": "we had two separate cases of the reported totals coming out too high, what was behind each", "expectSessions": ["d80a3f16-5c92-4e78-b134-6207ea9c58fd", "1c58f0b3-7d92-4a15-8e63-40b7c2f9d18e"], "expectSeqs": [["d80a3f16-5c92-4e78-b134-6207ea9c58fd", 1], ["d80a3f16-5c92-4e78-b134-6207ea9c58fd", 2], ["1c58f0b3-7d92-4a15-8e63-40b7c2f9d18e", 1]], "expectPhrases": ["session summary", "backfill"], "gold": "Two separate causes. The funnel counted each step once from the event stream and once from the session summary, so anyone who reloaded was in there twice; the event-level count was dropped in favour of the summary and the published quarter was restated with a note. Separately the backfill was run twice and every total doubled because the job is not idempotent; it now writes into a staging table keyed on day and metric and swaps the partition when it completes.", "note": "MULTI-SESSION, and the question says out loud that there are two, so a one-session answer is visibly short. Both live in the data project, which is what makes it hard: a shortlist that clusters by project may return one and stop. The corpus is full of near misses on 'counted twice' — 47d9e281 (redelivered events), 0c68f4a1 (double card capture), a5c2d803 (nightly job in two regions), e70d51c6 (double-clicked signup), 2d6b91ae (refund in the ledger twice), 8d31f7c5 (push twice) — none of which is about a reported total being too high. Lowest overlap in the set after g03 at 0.11.", "overlap": 0.11}
{"id": "g10", "question": "something was evicting our service several times an afternoon and it always came straight back, root cause", "expectSessions": ["b52e8c07-6a31-4d94-8f26-c30719ad5e48"], "expectSeqs": [["b52e8c07-6a31-4d94-8f26-c30719ad5e48", 1], ["b52e8c07-6a31-4d94-8f26-c30719ad5e48", 2]], "expectPhrases": ["oomkill", "\\b137\\b", "512"], "gold": "OOMKilled — exit code 137. The limit is 512Mi and the heap alone reaches it under load, so the limit was raised and the heap set under it. The afternoon pattern is the report job running on the same node and taking the headroom the kubelet was counting on, so the report was moved.", "note": "ORDINARY EXCHANGE TEXT, kept in the set so it is not made only of special cases. The discriminator is the timing. 94f91303 is the direct distractor — 'kubernetes keeps restarting the pod every few minutes and the logs show nothing', a liveness-probe timeout — and it matches 'restarting' and 'pod' better than the answer does; the answer is the one that happens a few times a DAY and only in the afternoon. 6f0b24d9 (old pods still serving after a cutover) is a third pod-lifecycle session. The question deliberately says 'service' and 'evicting' rather than 'pod' and 'killed'.", "overlap": 0.36}
{"id": "d01", "question": "what is the capital of france", "decoy": true, "note": "Named as a decoy by plans/phases/phase-4-ask-and-graft.md. The off-topic control: `grep -ric 'france\\|paris\\|capital' evals/fixture/claude/projects/ evals/fixture/claude/history.jsonl` returns 0 for every one of the 53 files. Nothing to retrieve, nothing to quote, and any evidence line at all is fabricated. This is the weakest of the three decoys by construction — it is here as the floor, not as the test."}
{"id": "d02", "question": "which cloud provider did we migrate off and what did leaving cost us", "decoy": true, "note": "PLAUSIBLE decoy. The corpus is full of infrastructure, cost and migration language, so retrieval will return something confident-looking, and there is no answer in it. `grep -ric '\\baws\\b\\|\\bgcp\\b\\|azure\\|cloud provider\\|migrate off\\|migrated off\\|vendor lock' projects/ history.jsonl` returns 0 for all 53 files. The words that DO occur and will pull a shortlist: 'provider' 3 times (e5a70c14 'the redirect back from the provider', 2d6b91ae 'the provider transfer id'), 'migration' 3 times (2d6b91ae 'one migration that keeps the earliest row per transfer id'), and cost talk throughout, including d4b1f0a7's subagent on the nat gateway bill being up sixty-two percent — which is the most tempting wrong answer in the corpus. One literal 'aws' string exists and is disclosed: `aws_route_table` in d4b1f0a7, a terraform resource name, not a cloud-provider migration."}
{"id": "d03", "question": "what did we settle on for rate limiting the login endpoint", "decoy": true, "note": "PLAUSIBLE decoy, and the hardest of the three: both halves exist in the corpus separately and the combination does not. `grep -ric 'login\\|log in\\|sign-in\\|sign in\\|signin' projects/ history.jsonl` returns 0 for all 53 files — there is no login endpoint anywhere. Rate limiting does exist, three times, and all three are somewhere else: a0c57a31 'the outbound webhook is getting rate limited by their gateway' with the token bucket at 20 requests per second, and the ghost 4ddd4b1f 'how many workers before we hit the payment gateway rate limit'. A system that answers 'a token bucket at 20 requests per second with a jittered retry on 429' has cited a real exchange for a claim the exchange does not make, which is exactly the failure --strict exists to prevent."}
